usage: flair spliceevents [-h] -m MANIFEST -g GENOME -f GTF [-o OUTPUT]
                          [-t THREADS] [--annot_basic ANNOT_BASIC]
                          [--junction_star JUNCTION_STAR]
                          [--junction_tab JUNCTION_TAB]
                          [--junction_bed JUNCTION_BED]
                          [--junction_support JUNCTION_SUPPORT]
                          [--ss_window SS_WINDOW] [--partition {yes,no}]
                          [--region_bed REGION_BED]
                          [--feature_support FEATURE_SUPPORT]
                          [--event_support EVENT_SUPPORT]
                          [--event_frac_of_tot EVENT_FRAC_OF_TOT]
                          [--junc_frac_of_event JUNC_FRAC_OF_EVENT]
                          [--quality QUALITY] [--trust_strand]
                          [--trust_junctions] [--keep_supplementary]
                          [--no_align_to_annot] [--keep_intermediate]

counts alternative splicing events, and their PSIs, of each sample directly
from bam files of long-read RNA-seq alignments, for data too fragmented or
variable in quality for flair transcriptome

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  -o OUTPUT, --output OUTPUT
                        output file name base (default: flair)
  -t THREADS, --threads THREADS
                        number of threads to run with (default: 12)
  --annot_basic ANNOT_BASIC
                        GTF annotation file of full-length transcripts, such
                        as GENCODE basic, whose first and last exons may also
                        be used as internal ones in some reads and still count
                        as first and last exons, and which unspliced reads are
                        fragments of, rather than those of -f. A comprehensive
                        annotation includes truncated transcripts, whose ends
                        are not real first and last exons
  --junction_star JUNCTION_STAR
                        splice junctions in STAR SJ.out.tab format, as STAR
                        writes when it aligns reads
  --junction_tab JUNCTION_TAB
                        deprecated name for --junction_star
  --junction_bed JUNCTION_BED
                        splice junctions as BED6 to BED9 with the number of
                        supporting reads in the score column, as intron-
                        prospector writes from a BAM of either short or long
                        reads. An alternative format to --junction_star, not a
                        different kind of evidence
  --junction_support JUNCTION_SUPPORT
                        minimum number of supporting reads to keep a junction,
                        from either --junction_star or --junction_bed; for bed
                        the score column holds the count (default: 2)
  --ss_window SS_WINDOW
                        window size for correcting splice sites (default: 10)
  --partition {yes,no}  whether to cut the regions reads are corrected and
                        events found in, in intergenic gaps of the annotation,
                        into pieces of about 5000 annotated exons, each owning
                        the reads that start in it. By default, yes for whole
                        chromosomes and no for --region_bed regions
  --region_bed REGION_BED
                        bed file of the regions to report events in, such as
                        the genes of interest; if not specified, all
                        chromosomes are used. Reads overlapping a region are
                        corrected over their whole length, and the events
                        reported are those inside a region: all their
                        junctions and exons, or, for the start, end, first and
                        last exon events, which count all of a gene's reads,
                        all its annotated junctions
  --feature_support FEATURE_SUPPORT
                        minimum number of reads of a junction, splice site or
                        end of an event in some sample (default: 2)
  --event_support EVENT_SUPPORT
                        minimum number of reads of an event in a sample for
                        its PSI to be calculated (default: 20)
  --event_frac_of_tot EVENT_FRAC_OF_TOT
                        minimum (total reads in splicing event)/(total
                        coverage over locus) to call event (default: 0.1)
  --junc_frac_of_event JUNC_FRAC_OF_EVENT
                        how alternative a junction, splice site or end must be
                        to make an event: its reads' part of the event's reads
                        at least this in some sample, and at most 1 minus it
                        in some sample. Set to 0 for max recall (default:
                        0.05)
  --quality QUALITY     minimum mapping quality of genomic alignments
                        (default: 1)
  --trust_strand        trust the stranding of the input reads and do not
                        attempt strand correction: reads keep the strand of
                        their alignment, and spliced reads are corrected only
                        with splice junctions on that strand, and are only
                        assigned to transcripts on that strand
  --trust_junctions     trust the strand of every input splice junction for
                        read correction, without checking splice motifs. By
                        default, an unannotated junction without a GT-AG
                        motif, such as GC-AG or AT-AC, is only weak evidence
                        of strand: a read stranded only by such junctions gets
                        its strand from gene identification, or is dropped
  --keep_supplementary  keep supplementary alignments
  --no_align_to_annot   skip the initial alignment to the annotated sequences
                        and correct reads only from the genomic alignment.
                        Slightly faster but less accurate when the annotation
                        is good
  --keep_intermediate   keep intermediate and temporary files for debugging

required named arguments:
  -m MANIFEST, --manifest MANIFEST
                        tab-separated manifest of sample name and path to its
                        sorted and indexed bam file aligned to the genome, a
                        line per sample
  -g GENOME, --genome GENOME
                        FASTA of reference genome, can be minimap2 indexed
  -f GTF, --gtf GTF     GTF annotation file, used for correcting reads and
                        assigning them to genes
